+A main-tree node is $(J,B)$, where $J$ is a product of bounded integer intervals and $B\subseteq X$ is a continuous box. Relax each integer interval to its real hull and call [[alg-factorable-mccormick-box-relaxation]] on the objective DAG and all constraint DAGs over $B$ times those hulls, passing the declared precision and interval format. A verified dual lower bound is valid on the original integer-feasible node by [[thm-factorable-dual-certificate-is-valid-box-lower-bound]]. The oracle may also return a feasible mixed-integer witness with a verified objective upper bound, a proof of emptiness, or an unavailable status. The latter never supplies a finite lower bound. Retain certified-empty nodes and their proofs in the ledger, but exclude them from further branching and lower-bound minima; they need no lower bound. Store lower bounds for terminal nodes and prune only nodes with a certified-empty proof; close a node only when its lower bound is at least $U-\varepsilon$, retaining that bound in the global ledger.
 
-Initialize the terminal ledger with the root $(Z,X)$, a FIFO queue containing that unevaluated root, remaining budget $N$, incumbent $U=+\infty$, and no incumbent witness. Each returned feasible witness with verified upper bound $U_x<U$ replaces the incumbent and sets $U=U_x$; retain the earlier witness on an equal bound. Keep unevaluated nodes in the FIFO queue ordered by creation. Generate lower integer children before upper integer children, and lower-half continuous children before upper-half children, appending children in this order. Before each node evaluation, if no call remains, return the current ledger with `budget-exhausted`; otherwise evaluate the oldest pending node and charge one call. When no node is pending, return infeasible if every terminal node is certified empty. If every nonempty terminal node has a valid lower bound, let $L$ be the least such bound; when $U$ is finite and $U-L\le\varepsilon$, return the feasible witness and global epsilon certificate. Retain bounds for closed nodes in this calculation. Otherwise choose an evaluated nonclosed node with least lower bound, breaking ties by creation order. If an integer interval has at least two values, choose the least-index such coordinate and split at $m=\lfloor(a+b)/2\rfloor$ into the disjoint children $[a,m]\cap\mathbb Z$ and $[m+1,b]\cap\mathbb Z$. Otherwise, if a continuous width is positive, split a longest continuous side at its exact midpoint, using least coordinate index ties. Children replace the parent in the coverage frontier and are evaluated before another split. A mixed singleton node that is neither certified empty nor closed by the gap test returns `inconclusive`.
